  "query_preview": "..\n\nHigh pressure and inbound ridge axis will control the weather today \ninto tomorrow. Surface high pressure expected to slide to the \neastern Great Lakes tonight while ridge axis ridge axis begins to \nslide into the central Great Lakes. The general southeast flow today \nis helping support a healthy cumulus field across most of southeast \nMichigan. Lack of deeper moisture and stability will keep the \nforecast free of precipitation through the remainder of today and \nthrough tonight. Lows tonight down into the 50s tonight. The \nincreasingly south to southwest flow tomorrow with the ridge axis and\neventually passage of it will lead. This will result in warm/moist \nadvection that will allow daytime highs on Tuesday to reach the low \n80s and surface dewpoints to climb above 60 degrees. \n\nHelping drive this warm/moist airmass into the region will be a \nPacific trough that is forecast to eject across the northern \nplains and central Canada. Low-mid level jet forcing along with the \nsystems associated cold front will lead to the next chance of \nwidespread rain and scattered thunderstorms Tuesday night through \nWednesday. Within the theta-e ridge will be a plume of deep moisture\nthat has PWAT values to around 2 inches and be the corridor of \nfocused convection. The southwest to northeast orientation of this \ncorridor will lead to PoPs increasing across the Saginaw Valley \nfirst on Tuesday night before greater upscale growth occurs through \nWednesday morning as the system propagates downstream into southeast \nMichigan over the course of Wednesday. Less favorable early day \ntiming and the potential for cloud cover may temper a stronger \ninstability response. Towards the southern Michigan border would be \nthe favored locations that greater instability may be able to \ndevelop. While shear will be favorable, mid-level lapse rates do not \nappear to be very steep. At this time, the severe threat appears \nlimited and held south of the state with general thunderstorms more \nlikely across southeast Michigan. Given the degree of moisture \nthough, locally heavy rainfall will certainly be possible. Current \nQPF guidance is to around a half inch across the Saginaw Valley with \nlesser amounts with southeast extent. In addition, winds will be \nelevated on Wednesday with increasing pressure gradient and gusts of \n25 to 30 mph will be possible. \n\nA trailing trough will help push the cold front out of the area by \nThursday morning. Post frontal airmass will bring cooler and less \nhumid conditions for Thursday and Friday. Temperatures back into the \n70s and dewpoints down in the 50s. Over the weekend, temperatures \nlook to rebound back to around 80 degrees within quasi zonal flow. \nPassing shortwaves to the north offer low end PoPs at this stage.",
